1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,082
Lesson 4.4.

2
00:00:02,462 --> 00:00:04,093
Sampling diagnostics.

3
00:00:05,217 --> 00:00:08,319
A correct sampler can be
useless if it is slow.

4
00:00:08,699 --> 00:00:12,780
The chain has the right stationary
distribution, but when did it get

5
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:16,441
there, and how many truly
independent samples did it give us?

6
00:00:16,821 --> 00:00:20,832
In this lesson we learn to
diagnose a run and two classic

7
00:00:20,832 --> 00:00:24,774
tricks to speed it up: annealing
and parallel tempering.

8
00:00:25,883 --> 00:00:28,407
Every chain starts in an arbitrary state.

9
00:00:28,787 --> 00:00:31,332
The first samples
remember that start and do

10
00:00:31,332 --> 00:00:33,760
not represent the stationary distribution.

11
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:37,374
That phase is called
burn-in, and it is discarded.

12
00:00:37,754 --> 00:00:41,558
A practical diagnostic is to
launch several chains from

13
00:00:41,558 --> 00:00:45,915
different starts: when their
averages overlap, burn-in is over.

14
00:00:47,017 --> 00:00:51,337
After burn-in, consecutive
samples are still correlated.

15
00:00:51,717 --> 00:00:55,515
As in lesson 2.6, the
integrated autocorrelation time

16
00:00:55,515 --> 00:00:58,883
says every how many sweeps
we get a new sample.

17
00:00:59,263 --> 00:01:03,766
The effective sample size is the
number of sweeps divided by that time.

18
00:01:04,146 --> 00:01:08,071
On a sixteen-by-sixteen lattice
at temperature 3.5, that time

19
00:01:08,071 --> 00:01:11,868
is about eight sweeps, and
thirty-eight thousand sweeps are

20
00:01:11,868 --> 00:01:15,150
worth about forty-five
hundred independent samples.

21
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:18,682
Now let's approach the
critical temperature.

22
00:01:19,062 --> 00:01:21,640
At 2.8, the autocorrelation time of the

23
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:24,879
magnetization rises to
about thirty-three sweeps.

24
00:01:25,259 --> 00:01:29,622
At 2.27 it shoots up to more than two
thousand, and the same thirty-eight

25
00:01:29,622 --> 00:01:33,388
thousand sweeps are worth barely
seventeen independent samples.

26
00:01:33,768 --> 00:01:37,214
This is critical slowing
down: large domains take a

27
00:01:37,214 --> 00:01:40,120
long time to reorganize
with local updates.

28
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:44,778
Near a phase transition, local
sampling becomes very expensive.

29
00:01:45,883 --> 00:01:50,513
If what we want is the minimum of the
energy, we can exploit temperature.

30
00:01:50,893 --> 00:01:56,068
Simulated annealing starts hot, where
the chain explores freely, and cools

31
00:01:56,068 --> 00:02:01,382
gradually to almost zero, where the
distribution concentrates on the minima.

32
00:02:01,762 --> 00:02:07,643
On a sixteen-by-sixteen spin glass,
a ten-sweep cooling ends at an

33
00:02:07,643 --> 00:02:13,701
energy of −1.25 per spin; with ten
thousand sweeps it reaches −1.37.

34
00:02:14,081 --> 00:02:18,447
Cooling more slowly gives better
minima, with diminishing returns.

35
00:02:19,550 --> 00:02:21,984
Parallel tempering runs
several replicas at

36
00:02:21,984 --> 00:02:24,249
different temperatures at the same time.

37
00:02:24,629 --> 00:02:30,240
Every so often it proposes swapping
the configurations of two neighboring

38
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:35,928
replicas, and accepts with probability
min(1, e^((β_i − β_j)(E_i − E_j))).

39
00:02:36,308 --> 00:02:39,562
That way, a configuration
stuck in the cold replica can

40
00:02:39,562 --> 00:02:42,756
climb to the hot one, cross
the barrier and come back.

41
00:02:43,136 --> 00:02:48,550
With β of 0.5 and 0.6, and an energy
difference of twenty, the swap

42
00:02:48,550 --> 00:02:53,398
is accepted with probability
e^(−2), about thirteen percent.

43
00:02:54,517 --> 00:02:57,938
In practice, always report the
autocorrelation time and the

44
00:02:57,938 --> 00:03:01,011
effective sample size, not
just the number of sweeps.

45
00:03:01,391 --> 00:03:07,138
A chip's energy per independent sample
is the energy per sweep times that time.

46
00:03:07,518 --> 00:03:10,884
And in optimization with
pbit hardware, the annealing

47
00:03:10,884 --> 00:03:14,314
schedule must slow down near
the critical temperature.

48
00:03:15,417 --> 00:03:18,767
Frequent mistakes: measuring
the autocorrelation on a

49
00:03:18,767 --> 00:03:21,739
fast observable while a
slow one has not mixed.

50
00:03:22,119 --> 00:03:26,113
Runs so short that the autocorrelation
time itself is underestimated.

51
00:03:26,493 --> 00:03:28,120
Cooling too fast.

52
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:32,558
And choosing temperatures so far
apart that swaps are never accepted.

53
00:03:33,683 --> 00:03:36,588
In summary: discard the
burn-in, measure the

54
00:03:36,588 --> 00:03:40,417
autocorrelation time and report
the effective sample size.

55
00:03:40,797 --> 00:03:44,957
Near the critical temperature,
local sampling becomes very slow.

56
00:03:45,337 --> 00:03:48,901
Simulated annealing uses
temperature to optimize, and

57
00:03:48,901 --> 00:03:51,926
parallel tempering uses
it to cross barriers.

58
00:03:53,050 --> 00:03:56,370
To practice: compute an
effective sample size.

59
00:03:56,750 --> 00:03:58,701
Explain critical slowing down.

60
00:03:59,081 --> 00:04:01,178
Derive the swap acceptance.

61
00:04:01,558 --> 00:04:03,706
And design a temperature ladder.
